  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1125 AM MDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery shows broad but low-amplitude\ntroughing over the Western half or so of the CONUS. Flow is\nrelatively weak over us with plenty of dry air aloft though\nsurface moisture remains seasonably robust with dew points in the\nupper 40s to upper 50s east. A weak system is departing the area.\nWith a lack of UL forcing, nearly all of us are expected to stay\ndry this afternoon. The exception will be toward the Hudspeth -\nCulberson County line as storms are expected to fire in that\nvicinity. Temperatures will be seasonable. We will see very\nsimilar conditions for tomorrow with a degree or two of warming.\nGFS and HRRR continue to show a single for isolated storms across\nHudspeth County (farther west in the GFS), so I added low POPs for\nthat area. Most of us remain dry. \n\nOur next UL system begins to affect the area on Thursday. A s/w\ntrough will strengthen off the Baja Coast before shifting ENE. \nThis pattern will draw moisture into Southern NM and Far West TX \nwhile UL support increases. Thus, showers and thunderstorms will \ndevelop/move north, with at least scattered coverage anticipated \nby Thursday afternoon into the overnight hours. We will see a \nquick break with this activity as the initial trough axis passes \nfollowed by a very small S/W ridge. This break does not last long \nthough as another ripple quickly follows Friday afternoon into \nearly Saturday morning with more showers and thunderstorms \nanticipated. For both days, modest CAPE (~500 J/kg) and shear (~20\nknots; slightly higher on Friday) should preclude a severe \nweather risk based on the LREF mean though it should be noted the \nEuro shows a little stronger CAPE (closer to 1000 J/kg) and shear \n(near 30 knots) for Thursday, which would lend to a non-zero \nsevere threat. Heavy rainfall and isolated flash flooding will be \nthe main concerns for Thursday and Friday.\n\nWe get a break for Halloween. The second ripple will be exiting\nthe area taking precip chances with it by day break with a s/w\nridge building in its wake. The rest of the day will feature \nclearing with temperatures near normal. There will be nothing \nspooky about the forecast that evening. \n\nThe Halloween ridge clears out quickly, and our next system begins\npushing into the area starting Sunday ahead of a large S/W trough \noff the SoCal Coast as eastern AZ and western NM enter the exit \nregion of the low's jet. Rain chances will slowly overspread the \narea from west to east beginning early Sunday morning for the AZ- \nNM border, reaching the eastern extent of our CWA by Sunday night.\nRain will be more stratiform with this event (isolated thunder), \nso the risk for severe will be none and flooding very low. This \nsystem will clear out sometime Monday, taking rain chances with \nit. It will also push a Pacific front through the area, and \ntemperatures will be below normal by the start of next week.",
